Yuku processes source code that may be attacker controlled, so it treats every input as untrusted.

## [Reporting a vulnerability]($section.id('reporting-a-vulnerability'))

Please do not open a public issue. Report privately through GitHub's [private vulnerability reporting](https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/yuku/security/advisories/new), or email [arshadpyaseen@gmail.com](mailto:arshadpyaseen@gmail.com).

We aim to acknowledge reports within 72 hours. Please allow a reasonable window for a fix before public disclosure, and we are glad to credit you in the advisory. Fixes ship against the latest version of each package.

## [Our practices]($section.id('our-practices'))

- **No third-party dependencies.** The npm packages depend only on each other and run no install scripts.
- **Provenance publishing.** Releases publish to npm with provenance through OIDC trusted publishing, with no long-lived tokens.
- **Hardened CI.** Every action is pinned to a commit hash, workflow tokens are read-only by default, and the workflows are checked by [zizmor](https://github.com/zizmorcore/zizmor) and [OpenSSF Scorecard](https://securityscorecards.dev).
- **Reproducible builds.** Installs use a frozen lockfile, and the one Zig dependency is pinned by content hash.
- **Memory safety.** A parse allocates everything in one arena, and the WebAssembly core runs sandboxed from host memory.
- **Fuzzing.** Millions of adversarial inputs per run, see [Testing](/testing/#fuzzing).
